Brigadier General Bun Pheng, Deputy Commissioner for Explosives and Firearms, said the guns that were burned today were wood-fired guns, some of which were shotgun shells, some were air guns, and had a total of 140 machine guns. These guns were collected from the people of the 9 districts / municipalities in the province who committed the crime and their keepers to shoot rats and other animals from 2017 onwards until now.
Banteay Meanchey Provincial Police Commissioner Major General Sithi Loh confirmed that the burning of second-hand guns today is a contribution to the safety of the village, commune, local people and society.
The commissioner also called on the people and young people to cooperate with the authorities and hand over the firearms and ammunition if found guilty, and if found guilty, will be punished according to law.
Electronic Cash Payments Rise During Kovid Outbreak 19
(Phnom Penh): Electronic cash payments have been on the rise. Increasingly, during the 199 epidemic, people changed their mindset from using cash payments to electronic payments to help reduce the spread of the 19 virus. Banknote term.
"The use of digital banking services can reduce the transmission of Kovid 19 by reducing the need for direct use of banknotes between individuals," said the National Bank of Cambodia.
At the same time, the National Bank of Cambodia also urges banks and financial institutions and institutions in Cambodia to encourage customers and enterprises to opt for electronic payment transactions instead of banknotes.
In fact, ACLEDA Bank Plc., Which has developed one of the leading digital banking services in Cambodia, has also been actively involved in providing easy, secure and fast electronic payments through its versatile ACLEDA Mobile. The use of electronic payments has played a large part in preventing the spread of Kovid 19 infection.
Ms. Sok Sophea, Chief Marketing Officer of ACLEDA Bank, said, "It is better for customers to come and pay digitally, which is a modern technology, because the new technology payment through ACLEDA Mobile helps customers not to "There are risks because customers do not touch banknotes that have not been disinfected before."
Ms. Sok Sophea stated that ACLEDA Bank Plc. Fully participates in the policy of the National Bank of Cambodia and she would like to encourage all customers to participate in the payment of other goods through the QL Code ACLEDA Mobile for ordering food products.
Returning to the business of people in many shops, as well as almost everywhere in Phnom Penh also embraced the new evolution of electronic payments.
Ms. Van Sokheng, the owner of a store that supplies baby supplies, said, "We have been offering customers the option to use paperless payment for three years. "Due to the Kovid 19 outbreak, the use of banknotes at its stores has increased by 30%."
She added that the banknotes were moving from person to person so quickly that it was not possible to determine whether the banknotes were held by a person with a positive Kovid-19 disease. This electronic system is the best choice for customers.
"During the 19 epidemic of Kovid, I saw an increase of 20-30% in non-bank payment transactions in my store compared to before the outbreak," she added. "As for the payment method, I always apply when I make a payment to the supplier."
Ms. Bun Sadeth, the owner of a motorcycle shop, said that the number of non-bank payments made at her shop increased by 60% compared to before the Kovid-19 outbreak.
"During the outbreak, I want customers to transfer money through the mobile banking app because we do not have to worry about meeting customers directly, just show our bank account number to customers and customers can send money," she added. Comes easily, which also saves time for counting money.
The benefits of non-banknote payments were even more evident during the 19 19 1940 Kovid outbreak, which encouraged customers to pay online, a practice that would reduce the direct handling of banknotes.
