The following is the press release of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration: The Ministry of Public Works and relevant officials want to apply for a new road traffic law
(Phnom Penh): Along with the preparation of the draft amendment on the Road Traffic Law, the working group of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and other stakeholders also expressed their joint intention to request the principle of unification from Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and Chairman of the National Road Safety Committee to draft a new road traffic law.
This discussion meeting was held for two days from 3 to 4 August 2021 in order to discuss the collection of inputs as well as to correct the meaning of words, phrases, spelling of the draft amendment to the Road Traffic Law. Comprehensive means that all road users strictly abide by traffic laws and reduce road traffic accidents to a minimum.
Please be informed that this amendment to the Road Traffic Law has been studied and discussed by a total of 17 expert working groups from May 2020 to January 26, 2021 and a number of discussion meetings. Two more meetings led by Senior Minister Sun Chanthol.
In this meeting, the meeting discussed the vocabulary, new amendments and new additions of the draft amendments to the Law on Road Traffic and agreed to incorporate the speed limit on the expressway into the sub-decree on speed limit before road operation. Speed too.
After several rounds of deliberations on the amendments to this law, 48 out of 92 articles were amended. To respectfully request the principle of unification to make a new law from Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol thanked the meeting and the working group for their discussions, providing inputs and comments in an effort to amend the law to achieve better results.
It should be noted that in the past, the tightening of the law on road traffic and the increase of fines by the Royal Government on road traffic offenses were not intended to make a profit, but the Royal Government wanted to see all road users get well. Safety, no accidents, injuries and deaths as these disasters left the disabled, widows, widows and orphans vulnerable to poverty.
