WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States on Friday announced sanctions against the Cuban police force and two of its leaders in response to what Washington sees as a crackdown by the Havana government. Protesters in the past. This was released by Reuters on Saturday, July 31, 2021.
The US Treasury Department said the new sanctions targeted two leaders of the Cuban police force as well as the national police force of the Cuban Interior Ministry and were a reaction to the crackdown on pro-democracy protests. Peaceful democracy in Cuba on July 11.
The new sanctions come as US President Joe Biden prepares to meet with Cuban community leaders in the United States, who have called on Washington to show more support for protests in Cuba.
Last week, the US Treasury Department also announced sanctions against the Cuban Defense Minister and a special force of the Cuban Interior Ministry for the same reason.
Demonstrations erupted in Cuba in July as Cuba faced a severe economic crisis, coupled with the global epidemic crisis COVID-19, as the Cuban president claimed that these problems were due to his country. US sanctions
General Knowledge: How are Hurricanes, Cyclones, and Typhoons Different?
Tropical storms can occur when the wind blows very quickly after receiving heat from warm seawater. Then, when the air cools down, it is pushed to one side by the hot air rising from below it. This cycle of phenomena caused storms. Tropical storms typically have winds of more than 117 kilometers per hour, while severe storms have even stronger winds.
At sea level, tropical storms can cause large waves. When these waves reach the land, they can flood large areas, including towns and cities. On land, strong winds can cause severe damage, destroying homes, knocking down trees as well as light poles, and even overturning vehicles.
Tropical storms usually disappear a few days after they hit the mainland because there is no longer warm sea water that can power it.
