Philippines closes Manila blockade to prevent Delta virus outbreak
Philippine Presidential Spokesman Harry Roque said in a televised address that closing Manila was a difficult decision but a good choice for all Filipinos. The spokesman added that the Manila metropolitan area, which has 16 cities and a population of more than 13 million, will be subject to strict closure measures from August 6 to 20. .
The $ 4 billion closure measure, which is expected to damage the national economy, will require residents to stay indoors only if necessary, with the permission of the authorities, such as to buy food or seek necessary services. .
Despite the sharp rise in the number of cases, which exceeded 10,000 a day in the past two weeks, the Thai Prime Minister has maintained that his government's efforts to address the epidemic COVID- 19 is not a problem and he said that many other countries can not control the epidemic as well.
The spread of the new COVID-19 delta, which is contagious, combined with the sharp rise in cases and deaths from the disease, has caused concern and criticism in the Thai public. In addition, reports of patients dying at home due to a lack of hospital beds, a lack of vaccines, and difficulty accessing health care have added to Prayuth's government.
