Commentary: Postage stamps commemorate the revolution as a witness to the history of the struggle of the Chinese Communist Party
(Beijing): Since the postage stamp, it has become a special way to record the most memorable moments in history. Although postage stamps are very small, they can reflect the economic and cultural features of a natural landscape as well as the customs, traditions and history of a country or region. Because it can be used in the post office and easy to store, it has become a precious memory of one generation after another.
Along with the dramatic changes in Chinese society, the postage stamp commemorating the revolution recorded each major event of the party and the state. The history of publishing postage stamps commemorating the revolution is also the history of social change in China.
In 1956, the Chinese Postal Service issued a commemorative postage stamp commemorating the "Eighth Congress of the Communist Party of China", the first time that China issued a stamp commemorating the party congress.
In 2016, on the occasion of the 80th anniversary of the victory of the Chinese Revolutionary Army, workers and peasants, the commemorative postage stamp was popular among stamp keepers, making people aware again. From the revolutionary events of that time.
On the morning of July 18, 2021, the Qingjiangpu Museum of Culture in Huaian, Jiangsu Province, held a special postage stamp exhibition commemorating the revolution to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the founding of the Communist Party of China. Pass through the exhibition of postage stamps, commemorating the revolution with historical value and document value to spread the spirit of taking turns not forgetting history and remembering its mission.
