Authorities Completely Rehabilitate Boeung Tumpun Canal, Collecting More Than 2,500 Trucks and Garbage
(Phnom Penh): The rehabilitation of Boeung Tumpun canal, which is nearly 4 kilometers long, to relieve the flooding in 5 districts of Phnom Penh, has now reached 100% after the team has completed the restoration work. In the past four months, more than 2,500 trucks, equivalent to more than 12,000 cubic meters, have been rehabilitated.
Mr. Duong Chansarath continued, “Boeung Tumpun canal is 3710 meters long and 30 meters wide from Stung Meanchey canal to Boeung Tumpun station on Street 371.
Restoration started on April 5, 2021 and was completed on July 23 by restoring 2510 trucks of garbage and floating grass equal to 12,050 cubic meters. This canal plays an important role in releasing water from Deum Sleng canal, French canal, especially liberating water from Khan Por Senchey, Khan Sen Sok, Khan Toul Kork and partly in Khan Chamkarmon and Khan 7 Makara.
