For the past 36 years, the Chinese government has been committed to the implementation of the "Convention on the Protection of the World Cultural and Natural Heritage of the World", as well as vigorously promoting the development of heritage conservation causes and has accumulated a wealth of experience. As well as contributing Chinese Buddhism, Chinese projects and Chinese strength to the cause of World Heritage conservation.
In 1992, the ancient Angkor site of Cambodia was inscribed on the World Heritage List. Separately, the Cambodian government and UNESCO started international activities in 1993 to preserve the Angkor site and received a series of financial and technological support from more than 10 countries.
As an initiator and the first participant in international activities to preserve Angkor, China has been involved in international activities to preserve Angkor since the 1990s to help Cambodia repair damaged temples and ancient structures. Damage. Currently, China has completed two temple restoration projects, Chao Sai Tevta and Takeo.
In November 2019, the project to renovate the former Royal Palace of Angkor Thom, the third phase of the Chinese-sponsored Angkor temple restoration project, officially began. The Royal Palace is the main temple in the Angkor resort. The Chinese team plans to use the temple for 11 years to restore the splendor of the Angkorian dynasty.
In addition to cooperating with Cambodia, Nepal and Kenya, China's cooperation on the conservation of World Heritage sites also extends to countries along the "Belt and Road". Technology and repair in Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Iran, India, Bangladesh, Myanmar and Uzbekistan.
