Previously, COVID-19 related travel guidelines for India and Pakistan were at the fourth, highest level. At level four, the US government advises people not to travel to those destinations, while at level three, Americans are advised to reconsider before deciding to travel.
However, the changes do not affect the travel ban imposed by the United States last May. The ban bans all foreigners who have been in India for the last 14 days from entering the United States.
The United States has imposed similar bans on South Africa, China, Iran, Brazil, the United Kingdom, Ireland and 26 European countries. But permanent residents of the United States, including their family members, and some foreigners, such as students, are exempt from the ban.
It should be noted that almost all passengers traveling to the United States by air must present proof of a COVID-19 negative test or proof of a cure for the disease before they can enter.
