Following is a press release from Battambang Provincial Administration: Civil Aviation Spokesperson: Investors at New Airports in Koh Kong, Battambang and Mondulkiri Delay Investment, Waiting for Better Transportation (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sin Chanserivutha, Undersecretary of State and Spokesman for Civil Aviation, confirmed that investors in the new airports in three provinces, including Koh Kong, Battambang and Mondulkiri, have postponed investment in construction. The airport is waiting for the transport sector to recover due to the spread of Kovid-19 disease.
Mr. Sin Chanserivutha stated, "Airport investors are concerned that the spread of Kovid 19 is serious, so they are not sure when it will get better. "When the transport recovers, this will cause them to delay investing in the airport."
Mr. Sin Chanserivutha pointed out that the concessionaire was concerned because when he invested, it was difficult to get a return on investment. So they postponed looking at the condition of Kovid 19 first.
He added that for Mondulkiri Airport, investment companies need some time to reconsider and recalculate the construction of the new airport, but generally do not see any obstacles, just need time to complete the paperwork. These are just some of the terms and conditions that apply.
Mr. Sin Chanserivutha said that the process of preparing the documents for the airport project in Mondulkiri may start at the end of 2021.
Please be informed that the construction of these three new airports has been announced by the Royal Government to invest to increase the potential of the economy and tourism.
Meanwhile, Mondulkiri Airport is planned to be built on an area of 300 hectares, which is under study and is estimated to invest more than 60 million dollars.
The Bek Chan Airport in Battambang province has been renovated to serve the tourism and transportation sectors for short and medium term. The project is still in the study phase. Australian researchers: "Lockdown can be difficult for people, but if Kovid-19 is not controlled, it will be even more difficult"
(Sydney): Australian and international researchers who have examined the response to epidemics in countries with and without blocking measures have found that blocking measures can actually affect human health. Yes, but the inability to control the epidemic of COVID-19 is even worse. This is according to a release by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Tuesday, July 20, 2021.
In fact, WMD suggests that countries with stricter measures to combat COVID-19 have lower mortality rates than in previous years, with researchers estimating that blocking measures could reduce annual mortality from 3 to 6 percent. The release comes as some Australians have complained about the closure of Sydney and Melbourne.
