Movement for Health of Cambodia reminds about the new health warning message on cigarette packs
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Movement for Health on July 20, 2021 reiterated that from August 1, 2021 onwards, all packs of cigarettes circulating in the market must have a new warning message in the form of pictures and It is a letter set by the Ministry of Health and according to the Prakas of the Ministry of Health, those health warning messages must be changed every two years.
Pursuant to this Prakas, the Ministry of Health has notified and provided a new image to the tobacco company since December 2020, for a period of 9 months from the deadline of August 1, 2021.
The Cambodian Movement for Health says restrictions on the printing of health warnings on cigarette packs regularly encourage smokers to quit and prevent women, youth and children from starting smoking. Smoking cessation contributes to the government in preventing the spread of Kovid 19 because smoking increases the risk of infection, and if smokers become infected, they are at greater risk than non-smokers.
Similarly, a study by the Australian Ministry of Health on the effectiveness of open cigarette packs shows that cigarette packs with health warnings, unadulterated images on cigarette packs are effective in making tobacco users aware of the effects of exposure. Smoking makes smokers start to worry about their health and makes smokers want to quit.
However, the effectiveness of the health warning message will decrease due to the long-term immunity to the same image, which is why the Ministry of Health has set to change the warning image to a new image every two years.
The Cambodian Movement for Health also said that failing to change the health warning message would reduce people's commitment to quitting. Tobacco is a dangerous product because half of all tobacco users fall ill, become disabled and die prematurely.
About 15,000 Cambodians die each year from tobacco-related diseases, it said. At the same time, about 1.4 trillion US dollars was lost due to tobacco use.
