Farmers in Bot Trang commune, Mongkul Borei district, Banteay Meanchey province set the amount of water required for higher yields
(Banteay Meanchey): After experiencing a series of floods and droughts in the past, farmers in Bot Trang commune, Mongkul Borei district, Banteay Meanchey province, who are engaged in farming, have set the amount of their fields in accordance with the amount of water to get the yield. High.
Heng Bun Socheat, a 32-year-old farmer living in Anlong Thnang village, Bot Trang commune, Mongkul Borei district, described that his area was flooded last year. Faced with the drought, he and other farmers have now shifted their farming practices, reducing the amount of land from large to small to match the amount of water available to maximize yields.
Obviously, as you can see, they are happy and hope that they will get more rice because the decision to limit the amount of rice from more to less makes them have enough water supply and rice grows well.
Separately, farmer named Touch Nay also said, "After determining the amount of rice to suit the amount of water, see that his rice grows well and hopes to get more. As for the market, they are still not worried about their farming to be more productive, they are happy.
Meanwhile, Mr. Rath Dasinang, Governor of Mongkul Borei District, said that the total amount of farmland of farmers in Mongkul Borei district is 52,000 hectares, and for rice cultivation at the beginning of this year seems to be favorable because there is enough irrigation water with rain. There will be frequent canals, rivers and ponds for farmers to irrigate rice paddies. Thailand will suspend submarine orders until the situation is relieved.
