Today, Sangvan, known to Cambodians all over the country as Samdech Pichey Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense of Cambodia, has gone through a difficult life for the cause of the nation and the motherland.
Sangvan's family and other villagers also inevitably oppressed these freedoms, which made Sangvan and the villagers resentful of such oppression. Phoeung village chief ordered the arrest of Sangvan's sister and handed her over to Komuy Sear, a police officer (police commissioner), for having sex in a lustful street. This made Sangvan very angry and strongly opposed to protecting his sister.
All those injustices made Sangvan, a young teacher, hate the oppression and rise up against it. Out of resentment and illegal use of power, the commune authorities arrested Sangvan and shot him dead on July 17, 1965. Coincidentally, Sangwan was shot dead and tried to escape into hiding in the forest. After this incident, Sangvan changed his name to "Tea Banh".
Later, Tea Banh's group joined the liberation of the country with the 2 December Front, led by Comrade Heng Samrin, Comrade Chea Sim and Comrade Hun Sen, liberating Koh Kong and Kampong Som provinces from the Khmer Rouge on January 17, 1979.
