French-language higher education agency holds regional workshop on "Integrity in Responsible Scientific Research"
(Phnom Penh): The French Higher Education Agency (AUF) organized a regional workshop on "Integrity in Responsible Scientific Research" on July 15, 2021, with the participation of approximately 255 people, all of whom are professors, leaders of higher education institutions and researchers in the Asia-Pacific region, Africa and Europe.
The workshop, chaired by Olivier Le Gall, an adviser to the French Office for Scientific Integrity of France, aims to provide some insights into a new context for researchers on honesty and fraud.
Faced with many challenges to our society of the early millennium, science has meaning to speak. But there are also some questions: Do scientific methods really belong to our people and political representatives? To what extent do they have confidence in science? Is this trust created by dealing with fraud? ”
Recently, French research is gaining a consensus of scientific honesty based on conscience, meaning "elephant in the middle of a room." The foundations of ethics, integrity and professional ethics have become the three pillars of responsible science.
Olivier Le Gall emphasizes that honesty is not an issue, but that scientific methods and methods must be applied honestly so that science can live up to the expectations of responsible scientists of the scientific community. .
This is the fifth time that the French Higher Education Agency (AUF) has organized online in the Asia-Pacific region within the framework of assisting member higher education institutions to improve the quality of their training, or distance learning. At the end of the presentation, the participants asked a lot of questions about fraudulent work as well as how to protect their work and asked for other advice.
(Phnom Penh): A herd of vultures, including large vultures and small vultures, was recently photographed by researchers at the Ministry of Environment in Cambodia's wildlife sanctuary.
Regarding the presence of the endangered herd in the world, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, confirmed to Fresh News on July 16, 2021 that the presence of many cubs is It is a new hope for conservationists and for the world's biodiversity, as storks are listed on the IUCN Red List as the world's most endangered species.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra also mentioned the history of squirrels that have been present in the forest areas in South and Southeast Asia and Sri Lanka. The number of bulls has been studied by more than 80% in the last century, and the number of bulls in the world has decreased by about 90% since the middle of the 20th century.
The spokesman said that according to a study by wildlife experts in Cambodia, there was a decrease of more than 50% during the period between 1960 to early 1990. That number has been found to be declining further so far. Although studies have shown that the number of vultures in Cambodia is declining in line with the global declining trend, the population of vultures is found in almost all protected areas.
