(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, said that if the countries of the world do not adhere to multilateralism, the world will not be able to overcome the epidemic now and in the future. .
The Cambodian diplomat made the remarks at a mid-term ministerial meeting of the Non-Aligned Movement on "Non-Aligned Movement at the Point of Multilateral Efforts in Response to Global Challenges" held from 13th to 14th of July 2021.
In his statement, Prak Sokhon stated: "For Cambodia, only within the framework of a true multilateralism will the international community be able to address these key challenges. "The only thing that describes multilateralism for its own benefit without recognizing the pluralism of the world is just talk."
"True multilateralism means accepting differences and turning rivals into peaceful and honest competition," he said. Without these, we will not be able to overcome current and future epidemics, we will not be able to slow down climate change and ecology, and we will not be able to manage new technologies in the context of competitors. "That we are experiencing today."
Today, among the major challenges that have plagued the world in the last two years, including the geopolitical rivalries of the superpowers, the outbreak of the devastating 19-kovid epidemic is the worst phase of the crisis. Health, economy - social affairs and humanitarian. Increasing recovery of natural and climate-related disasters and the possible shift towards a digital economy.
According to Prak Sokhon, Cambodia firmly believes that in response to these global challenges, multilateral cooperation efforts should be fundamentally incorporated into regulatory systems in line with the Bandung Principles. Baku 2019 and the UN Charter.
The global challenges associated with the arrival of the Fourth Industrial Revolution will require us to work together on technology transfer and technology sharing, not just between developed and developing countries. Providing such technological support plays an important role in maintaining the economic sustainability of each non-aligned member state during the transition to a global digital economy.
Attracting technological investment in infrastructure, education and health will help boost economic growth.
