(Phnom Penh): Mr. Oknha Song Saran, President of the Cambodian Rice Federation, said that the transportation problem is still an obstacle to the decline of Cambodian rice exports to international markets due to 5 to 10 times higher shipping costs than before. There is still a problem with Kovid 19.
This statement was made by Duke Song Saran in an interview on the program "Special Guest of the Week" on the topic of "Decrease in Cambodian rice exports to international markets."
The tycoon stated that some developed countries have opened their economies, making them in high demand in those countries. In general, not only foodstuffs, but almost all goods are affected by the economic recovery, affecting heavy goods such as rice, which is difficult to find in most containers. Taken to transport light goods to meet the needs of those countries.
"The demand for our orders is that we have a crisis in transportation, unable to find containers and shipping costs have increased five to ten times," he said. "If we look at the location and prices in 2019, especially to the European Union, there was an increase of almost 10 times compared to before the outbreak of Kovid 19."
"The increase in shipping costs makes some buyers hesitant because the price of rice is almost equal to the shipping cost," he added. In fact, if you look at rice per container, it is about 15,000 to 20,000 dollars, and if we transport it, we have to pay 7,000 to 10,000 dollars, so almost 50% of the price is paid to the company. "Delivery."
He said, however, that there are still buyers, but there is a decline because the buyers who dare to pay at that level are less, so some countries he has to lack food to supply his people, he also makes prices. Inflation has risen sharply in their countries, such as the United States, the European Union and some countries with high inflation during the economic recovery.
He said that in the first six months of 2021, the Rice Federation, the Ministry of Commerce and the Ministry of Agriculture tried to avoid the risk of decline by looking at the Chinese market. In the past six months, Cambodia has exported more than 50 percent of Cambodia's total rice exports. The increase to China is helping a large number of sectors, and without the Chinese market, the Asian market, Cambodia would be very difficult.
The tycoon said that Cambodia is just waiting for container shipments to return to its original state, if there are containers, Cambodia will be able to export immediately, especially in the second quarter of this year.
