General knowledge: People came to live in Angkor at least 3800 years ago
(Siem Reap): Although Angkorian civilization was known for its glory from the 9th to the 15th century, our Khmer ancestors came to live in Angkor from prehistoric times, with archaeologists excavating Human bones dating back 3,800 years, along with some of the secrets that remain with the tombs.
Evidence is housed in the "Norodom Sihanouk-Angkor Museum", located in Sangkat Slor Kram, Siem Reap, Siem Reap Province. In this museum, there is an exhibition of tombs and skeletons (artificial), skulls, pottery, jewelry and utensils of prehistoric people from the excavations found during 2004-2005.
Kun Phally, a guide at the Norodom Sihanouk-Angkor Museum, said that the excavations at the tombs and skeletons of the prehistoric people were at the "Koh Meas" prehistoric site, a small island. One is in Teuk Thla baray (western baray), about 1,300 meters west of the western Mebon temple.
"Koh Ta Meas station is the oldest prehistoric station in Siem Reap," Phally said. The station dates back to the Bronze Age, which is divided into two phases, the first phase dating from 1800-1600 BC and the second phase dating from 1600-1000 BC.
At this ancient prehistoric site, archaeologists have unearthed tombs, tombs, human skeletons, pottery, bronze and metal jewelry, and other Bronze Age utensils. They even found offerings or offerings that the people of the generation offered to the dead, including pig heads, grain, vases, and other utensils.
Archaeologists are still struggling to gather enough evidence before reaching the final conclusions about the cause of death. Leukemia. The study was conducted scientifically and ethnically.
TOKYO (Reuters) - Japanese authorities on Saturday ordered more than 120,000 people in some parts of the southern provinces to evacuate to safer areas set up by the state over fears of heavy rains. . This is according to local media quoted by Reuters on Saturday, July 10, 2021.
Japan's NHK television reported that the Japan Meteorological Agency has declared high rainfall in some prefectures on the island of Kyushu, and local authorities have ordered people to evacuate to safer places. .
The evacuation order came just over a week after heavy rains hit and caused floods and landslides in Atami, killing at least nine people. 20 people are missing.
