(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has issued an order to the authorities along the border with Thailand to try to prevent the new Kovid 19 virus from infecting the community.
Samdech Techo Prime Minister issued such an order while Samdech led the meeting of the Council of Ministers this morning, July 9, 2021.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed concern that at present, 19 hundred workers returning from Thailand are found to be positive for 19 hundred Kovid diseases every day, especially at the border checkpoints in Banteay Meanchey and Oddar Meanchey provinces. In order to prevent the transmission of disease from workers from Thailand into the community, Samdech Techo advised to ensure that all workers from Thailand enter the community directly.
It should be noted that in order to prevent the import of Kovid 19 from Thailand through these workers, the Royal Government of Cambodia has decided to conduct a quick test at the border and require them to do a 14-day patrol to be allowed to go to the destination.
National Center for Health Promotion of the Ministry of Health: The use of tobacco products is not good for the people at all, especially during the epidemic of Kovid.
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Chhea Chhadaphea, President of the National Center for Health Promotion of the Ministry of Health and a member of the Tobacco Products Control Commission, said that the use of tobacco products is not good for the people at all, especially during the epidemic. Kovid-19 disease itself.
According to a recent press release, Dr. Chhea Chhadaphea requested all ministries, institutions and capital-provincial levels not to receive support from the tobacco industry, which has already been banned by sub-decree.
She warned of legal action against tobacco companies that intend to promote their products by helping society to reap the benefits of the sponsorship. The warning to take legal action against the tobacco company after the tobacco company posted pictures on social media related to its sponsorship activities to some ministries and institutions in order to promote their products.
"The tobacco industry is trying to do that to show that it is working with society to help, especially with the current Kovid-19 disease, but her real intention is to advertise," she said. He said it was a good deed of his company in society and this activity was prohibited by law.
She claimed that if all the tobacco industry companies were involved in this activity, they would face legal action. She said that the use and exposure to secondhand smoke, it has a serious impact on health, leading to cardiovascular disease, lung cancer, diabetes, high blood pressure and many other diseases.
Mr. Ngy Mean Heng, Director of the Phnom Penh Municipal Health Department, has announced that he will not accept any material support from the tobacco industry company in order to comply with the Royal Government's sub-decree and the policy of the Ministry of Health. He added that in the past, the Phnom Penh Health Department has never received any material support from the tobacco industry.
It should be noted that the Royal Government of Cambodia issued a sub-decree on the organization and functioning of the Tobacco Products Control Committee on July 14, 2017, with all relevant ministries and institutions and all capital-provincial administrations as members. Article 3 of this sub-decree makes it clear that the members of this committee are individuals who are not involved in conflicts of interest with the tobacco industry or companies.
It should be noted that the use of tobacco kills about 40 Cambodians every day and costs about 649 million US dollars per year in Cambodia.
