International Analysis: Why is China mobilizing troops near the Myanmar border?
Rangoon: Many media outlets recently reported that China had mobilized troops in Jiegao, on the border with Muse in Myanmar's Shan State. Myanmar has been in turmoil since the events of February 1, which the West, especially the United States, considered a coup to overthrow Aung San Suu Kyi-elected civilian government.
In September 2020, China also decided to take special action along the border with Myanmar, but at that time China closed its border with the Southeast Asian country due to the COVID-19 outbreak.
* Impact of the coup
The events of February 1, which the West, especially the United States, considered a coup to overthrow Aung San Suu Kyi-elected civilian government, sparked protests across Myanmar, including Attacks on Chinese factories in Rangoon and other cities
In particular, observers in Myanmar believe that the mobilization of troops is in readiness to protect China's interests, maintain stability in the border area, as well as be ready to prevent the conflict from spreading or the influx of refugees into the territory. China: At a time of crisis in Myanmar, there seems to be no sign of a way out.
China has repeatedly claimed that it has always supported resolving the crisis in Myanmar peacefully and through ASEAN mechanisms. ASEAN has also held special meetings with Myanmar's military chief, Ming Aung Leng, and even reached an agreement calling for a ceasefire and an immediate end to all violence. But so far they have not seen any signs of relief or positive.
