The last Arctic ice sheet is at greater risk than expected due to global warming.
The upper regions of northern Greenland and Canada are nicknamed because, although the amount of ice there varies with the seasons, scientists used to understand that during the summer they still Can maintain a large enough presence because it is thick and does not easily disappear like other parts of the Arctic Ocean.
However, during the summer of 2020, a portion of the eastern part of the region, studied by researchers from Washington State University in the United States, was observed to have lost about 50 percent of its ice mass. The reason is because of climate change, it has been making the ice there thinner year by year.
As global warming continues to deplete ice levels in other parts of the Arctic Ocean, the findings could be even more of a problem for glacial-dependent species. There, the last glacier is considered their main refuge, while other parts of the Arctic are completely free of ice.
