Russia sees Europe as more important than Asia
So like it or not, with a focus on maintaining and expanding influence in Europe, Russia is well aware that competition in Europe with NATO and in Asia with China is not a good option. Instead, Russia needs China for its geopolitical interests.
Russia considers China more important than Asia
Russia has stepped up diplomatic ties with China since tensions with the United States and the European Union over its annexation of Crimea from Ukraine in 2014. But at the same time, Moscow does not seem to be strengthening or enhancing relations with other countries in the Asia-Pacific region.
Russia needs the Chinese market to free itself from US and European sanctions, while China needs Russia, the world's second-largest military power, to repress the United States.
Russia's limited relations with ASEAN
Moscow has been more actively involved with ASEAN since 2016 through the establishment of the ASEAN-Russia Summit, and Russia has also achieved some success in this regard, including increasing arms sales and trade. And energy cooperation with ASEAN member countries.
