(Ratanakkiri): The park ranger is the most important agency of the Royal Government of Cambodia in protecting the natural resources of forests and wildlife in national parks and protected forest areas. The Ministry of Environment currently has 1,200 park rangers guarding protected areas.
Mr. Thon Sokhon is one of more than 1,200 park rangers guarding the natural resources in the Vern Sai-Siem Pang National Park of the Ratanakkiri Provincial Department of Environment. Mr. Sokhon has spent 20 years in the cause of protecting this natural resource to preserve it for future generations.
In this mission to protect natural resources, Mr. Sokhon and many of his team went through many obstacles and difficulties. Mr. Thon Sokhon reminded, "I joined in 2000. The first mission patrolled Virachey National Park for 15 days. "The first mission was very difficult, up and down the mountain, carrying more than 20 kilograms of backpacking materials."
The natural resource defender recalled that the hardship almost forced him to give up the task. But after meeting with the team to discuss the mission and talk about the need to protect this natural resource, he changed his mind and continued this mission until today.
Mr. Sokhon has been working as a protector of forests and other natural resources since he was 23 years old and now he is 42 years old. During the mission for the past 20 years, Mr. Sokhon has encountered many memories in this work, especially encountered and experienced many difficulties.
Mr. Thon Sokhon recalls a memory while working by hanging a hammock in a stream without expecting rain. Only at night did it rain, causing the water to flow and flood the material. Out of water.
"I think when we spend the night in this stream it is very dangerous sometimes. So I want to show that our difficulties are like this. Sometimes we also face some chronic diseases such as malaria and typhoid fever. "So we used to have problems when we were patrolling the area."
The park ranger added that the mission encountered obstacles when he completed the previous mission was very difficult and encountered obstacles such as tying hammocks, burning fires, sometimes falling asleep and losing fire. There used to be a hammock fire. He also fought bears, used to swim across streams for two days.
Despite the hardships of life as a natural resource protector for the past 20 years, Thon Sokhon still loves and is loyal to this work to protect Cambodia's natural resources for future generations. He also considered this work a source of pride for him.
Mr. Thon Sokhon added, "I am proud to have served as a ranger as a ranger to help protect the natural resources for our descendants."
