(Phnom Penh): A strong new stupa structure first appeared on Tuol Theat site in Rong Cham village, Kampong Trabek district, Prey Veng province, after a request from Cham commune authorities to meet the needs of survivors from The Khmer Rouge regime to preserve the remains of victims who died during the Khmer Rouge regime and to commemorate the events that took place in Cham commune at that time.
The construction of this stupa is in response to the Compensation Scheme of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, which has ruled in its judgment since Case 002/01 to provide collective compensation for the purpose of recognition. Officialize the victims of the Khmer Rouge regime, reduce the harm and suffering that the victims experienced and maintain collective memory, and enhance the dignity of the victims.
DC-Cam, in collaboration with local authorities in Cham commune, provided a public site for construction, agreed to the victims' request, and began construction of a stupa and a slogan commemorating Khmer Rouge history in mid-May. 2021 and completed on July 2, the same year with $ 10,000 at Tuol Theat in Rong Cham village, Kampong Trabek district, Prey Veng province.
Before the Khmer Rouge regime, Tuol Theat was a public place where villagers buried the bodies of their dead relatives. Tuol Theat is now a place where villagers come to pray and dedicate to their relatives during Pchum Ben, New Year and Memorial Day on May 20 every year. This location is in Rong village, Cham commune, Kampong Trabek district, Prey Veng province.
Mr. Mom Ven said that he and all members of the Association after the Khmer Rouge Survivors will work together as a whole to maintain this compensation permanently and try to find ways to create some programs that It is the key to connecting the history of the Khmer Rouge with post-Khmer Rouge survivors and the younger generation.
