Cambodian diplomat tells Special Rapporteur that democracy is not a short-distance run, but a long-distance run
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, told Professor Vithit Montaboun, Special Rapporteur on Human Rights in Cambodia, that democracy is not a race. Short, but it's a long run.
The head of the Cambodian diplomatic corps said this during a video conference with Mr. Montaboun on July 1, 2021. Discussions focused on three main topics: 1: International and regional cooperation, 2: Kovid 19 and its consequences, and 3: Democracy and citizenship.
According to the results of a meeting obtained by Fresh News on July 2, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs stated: "On democracy and the people, the Deputy Prime Minister stressed that any assessment of Cambodia should be remembered. Where did that assessment come from and where is it at the moment? "Mechanisms have been set up to build closer partnerships between the government and civil society organizations."
"Democracy is not a short-distance race, it is a long-distance race. All legal and administrative measures are strengthened and implemented within the legal boundaries. The charges were filed in court in connection with criminal offenses and violations of the Constitution. "Defendants have the full opportunity to hear and deny any charges that are part of the right to a due process hearing."
Considering himself a good friend of Cambodia, Professor Vithit Montaboun emphasized his deep respect for Cambodia. He praised Cambodia's comprehensive cooperation in human rights mechanisms, such as the ratification of various international human rights conventions, participation in treaty institutions, periodic review mechanisms and special rapporteurs, and significant progress. Note in economic and social rights.
This is the sixth video conference that Professor Vitit Montaboun has met with Cambodian government officials since taking office. The new Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Cambodia met with dignitaries and representatives from the Ministry of Interior, the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, the Ministry of Health, the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, and the Ministry of Justice.
Prof. Vitith thanked the Royal Government of Cambodia for facilitating an open and constructive dialogue that demonstrates Cambodia's commitment to good cooperation in his new term.
